Embeddings: Turning Text into Meaning that we can Measure

Today, learning about making text measurable — turning sentences into numbers so the computer can compare their meaning.
This is the gear between "chat" (Day 8) and "chat that knows the documents" (RAG, Day 11).

Briefing:
An embedding is a list of numbers (a vector) that represents the meaning of a piece of text.
Similar meanings → vectors sit close together. Unrelated meanings → vectors sit far apart.

"login failed"         → [ 0.12, -0.88,  0.31, ... ]   (~768 numbers)
"authentication error" → [ 0.14, -0.85,  0.29, ... ]   ← very close to above
"banana bread recipe"  → [-0.91,  0.05,  0.77, ... ]   ← far away
The magic: meaning becomes geometry. A computer can't compare meanings, but it can compare numbers. Embeddings are the bridge — once text is a vector, "are these similar?" becomes plain math.
Same direction = same meaning. That's the whole concept.

